From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Johannes Stein <johannesstein@freeze-dev.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 05:51:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 132/140] aarch64: count a far tbz or tbnz in bytes, with the
 exact reach

The tbz and tbnz relaxation (patch 0131) counted the code in instructions
with a margin of about 1,200: it gave inline data (.ascii) no size, so a tbz
across it stayed out of reach, lost a label at offset 0 (stored as nil, read
as missing), compared the label from one layout with the branch from
another, and counted two references too many on each rewrite.

It now counts bytes: instructions, constants, strings and the most padding
an alignment can add, so a counted distance is never shorter than the real
one. A tbz or tbnz is rewritten only when its label is outside the encoding's
reach, -32768 to +32764 bytes; labels are stored one higher; the code a
rewrite adds is left out of the count until the next pass; and the b takes
over the reference to the far label.

tests/test/opt/ttbz2.pp branches across 36000 bytes of data, back to the
first instruction of a procedure, and twice in a row, where rewriting the
first moves both ends of the second. ttbz1's branch spans about 88 KiB of
code (22,487 instructions), not the 300 KiB the earlier message gave.
